<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belarusians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Belarusians are an East Slavic people who form the majority of the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and live in neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Their lands formed the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, where Ruthenian, an ancestor of their language, was used in government. Between the world wars they were divided between Poland and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and an independent Belarus emerged in 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belarusians.jpg" alt="Belarusians" width="320" height="144" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Allice Hunter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Belarusian_Diaspora_in_the_World.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
